  "summary": "Rejects incoming Bitcoin Core RPC requests containing an `Origin` header before checking authentication. This prevents browsers from executing cross-origin RPC calls using cached HTTP credentials.",
  "problem": "When a user logs into Bitcoin Core RPC via a web browser, the browser caches the HTTP authentication credentials. Other websites visited in that browser can issue cross-origin requests to the local RPC port; although the browser's same-origin policy prevents reading the response, the requests still execute on the node with cached credentials.",
  "evidence": [
    "Commit cbe4698cb5 explains: 'After a browser authenticates to RPC, another page permitted to reach the endpoint may cause calls to execute using cached credentials, even when it cannot read the response.'",
    "Description cites real-world examples of web applications prompting direct browser connections to local RPC (Voltage Block Clock, PR #12040 discussion)."
  ],
